course: dictionary
lesson: 12265
title: Contend: deal with, compete, or argue
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: «Contend» is a verb with three main meanings. To contend with something means to have to deal with a problem or difficulty, as in «Farmers have to contend with drought.» To contend for something means to compete with others to win it.
explain: The third meaning is more formal. To contend that something is true means to say firmly that it is true, especially in an argument. «She contends that the plan will fail» means she firmly believes the plan will fail. Look at the words after contend to find which meaning you have.
explain: The verb changes with time and person: contend, contends, contended, contending. Use contends after he, she or it, and contended for the past. Remember the pattern: contend with a problem, contend for a prize, contend that a claim is true.
fact: Contend means to deal with something hard, to compete for something, or to argue firmly that something is true.
fact: «Contend with» is followed by a problem or difficulty, such as «contend with floods».
fact: «Contend for» is followed by something you want to win, such as «contend for the cup».
fact: A «contender» is a team or person with a real chance to win.
fact: In an essay or debate, «X contends that…» introduces someone's claim, so you can report their view fairly.
exercise: «Five teams are contending for the cup.» What does contending for the cup mean? | Dropping the cup | Giving the cup away | Cleaning the cup | *Competing to win the cup
exercise: «She contends that the plan will fail.» What does contends mean here? | Wrestles with a plan | Forgets the plan | *Says firmly that something is true | Hopes for a party
exercise: Find and copy one word that means a team with a real chance to win. | plan | drought | cup | *contender
test: Which word completes the sentence? «Mia ___ that the bridge is safe, but nobody has checked it.» | contend | *contends | contending | contended
test: Which word completes the sentence? «Yesterday the twins ___ for the prize.» | contending | contend | contends | *contended
exam: Which sentence uses contend to mean firmly arguing that something is true? | «Lucy contends the bus stop is near the park.» | «Lucy contends with a bad cold all week.» | *«Lucy contends that the bus is always late.» | «Lucy and Sam contend for the lead in the race.»
